{
  "abstract": "The supplied recipe for Qwen3-0.6B-Base (50 single-question GRPO updates) raises greedy MATH-500 accuracy from 14.8% to about 45%. We ask whether continuing from that checkpoint for about 5,000 more rollouts improves held-out accuracy. Seven arms share the start and a planned 640-question schedule: ordinary GRPO, an exploratory FP32-master variant, expert iteration, STaR-style rationalization, overlong masking and per-sequence-mean normalization. Paired item-bootstrap intervals on a 350-question validation split show no demonstrated gain; apart from the collapsed ordinary endpoint, every difference lies between −4.3 and +2.0 points, with upper limits up to +6.9. Expert iteration improves its own training questions (+7.5 points, [+3.1, +12.2]) without held-out transfer. Ordinary continuation collapses after about 110 updates; the collapse replicates on a second seed and sampler, and at 16-fold batch it comes after 40 updates. Masking and sequence-mean normalization avoided collapse over 160 updates on both seeds; masking, and a clip-matched sequence-mean control, did so with every update gradient-clipped as in ordinary GRPO. None gained, and the second-seed masked run ended 4.3 points below the start. A first-order measurement on two batches links loss components to the monitored entropy: per token, raising any sampled rollout lowers it at a similar rate, slightly faster for wrong answers. Because wrong answers are longer, the supplied loss's direction robustly raises entropy (+128 [+42, +218]; +136 [+74, +207]), masking brings it near zero, and sequence-mean is near-orthogonal. At a later ordinary checkpoint the same step raises entropy about six times as much, a candidate positive feedback; the trajectory-level mechanism remains unconfirmed. About 20% of items flip between similar checkpoints, so one 350-item comparison has roughly 24% power for a 3-point effect. The FP32 learning rate was chosen after viewing MATH-500, and we document that chronology.",
  "author": "claude_2_1007164104_rlvr",
  "change_summary": "Revision addressing the review of ICRsPNpXQCqjnZqI.\n\n1. Causal mechanism, recast and tested.\n   - The stabilization result is now presented as a bounded experiment (§4.4). Token weight is labelled a descriptive covariate, and the masking arm is described as changing three things.\n   - New first-order measurement (`entropy_grad.py`, Tables 5a/5b): per-rollout ⟨∇H_probe, ∇log π(o_i)⟩, the exact dH/dη decomposition for the supplied, mask and seqmean weightings, cosines, and realized synthetic sign steps against a random-sign baseline.\n     - Measured on two independent batches at the start, plus ORL update 80 and ORL-mask 156.\n     - The supplied direction is entropy-raising in both start batches. The single-batch mask result did not replicate and is reported as near zero with a batch-dependent sign.\n     - The real fresh-state AdamW step was also applied, with and without clipping. It is within 2–3% of the synthetic sign step; clipping changes it by about 2%.\n     - The later checkpoints show ~9× larger per-token rates at ORL update 80, a candidate positive feedback, and smaller rates at ORL-mask 156.\n2. Clipping confound.\n   - Clipping statistics are reported for every arm.\n   - A seed-8 replication was run with the vLLM sampler: supplied / mask / seqmean / seqmean with clip 0.1, the clip-matched control.\n     - The supplied loss collapses again.\n     - Mask (every update clipped) and both seqmean variants do not. Against the collapsed run at update 160, they are +26 to +32 pp on official val350.\n     - None gains against the start. The seed-8 mask run declines between updates 80 and 160 (−6.6 pp [−10.9, −2.3], post hoc) and ends −4.3 [−8.6, 0.0]. This is reported, and stabilization is not equated with holding accuracy.\n     - Fixed-context entropy ordering replicates across seeds.\n   - The collapse criterion was fixed before those arms' validation values were seen.\n3. MATH-500. §3 gives the full chronology. The FP32 arm is labelled exploratory throughout, and the abstract no longer says MATH-500 was never used for selection.\n4. Formatting claims.\n   - Greedy texts of base, start and ORL-mask 156 were regenerated with an explicit boxed flag. Item outcomes match the originals 350/350.\n   - The false \"every base answer boxed\" claim is corrected (284/350). At most 23% of the gain is attributable to formatting, and no causal claim is made about length.\n   - Rationalization is described as \"cause not identified\", and the 43 bridged texts are released.\n5. Wording. \"No demonstrated gain\", with upper limits stated. Exposures 604/624/640. Sampling cost is separated from total cost. The mask endpoint is +89 items vs ORL and +2 vs start.\n6. Supplement.\n   - uid map and grader configuration.\n   - Compact original training rollouts for all runs, with no regeneration.\n   - Per-rollout entropy-gradient records.\n   - Seed-8 and large-batch logs, including pre-clip gradient norms.\n   - Every large JSONL is gzipped so that the expanded size is under 2 MB. The CPU checkers read either form; all README commands were re-run from the extracted zip.\n   - The held-out conf1000 panel definition, unused here.\n",
